    "referenceAnswerEn": "(a) Occupancy = 200/250 = 80%; average room rate = 360 000/200 = 1 800 dollars.\n(b) RevPAR = 360 000/250 = 1 440 dollars (or 80% × 1 800).\n(c) The second hotel’s RevPAR = 95% × 1 500 = 1 425 dollars, slightly below the first hotel’s 1 440. Occupancy alone records how many rooms were sold and nothing about the price obtained. The second hotel’s far higher occupancy looks like stronger performance, yet its yield is lower, because it bought occupancy with a lower rate.\nRevPAR is the industry’s central measure precisely because it carries both price and volume: by keeping unsold rooms in the denominator it puts \"sold cheaply and full\" and \"priced high and half empty\" on one scale. Occupancy alone shows how much was sold but not for how much.",
    "contentEn": "A hotel has 250 rooms. On one night it sells 200 rooms for total room revenue of 360 000 dollars.\n(a) Find the occupancy rate and the average room rate.\n(b) Find the revenue per available room (RevPAR).\n(c) Another hotel that night has 95% occupancy at an average rate of 1 500 dollars. Compare the two RevPAR figures and explain why comparing occupancy alone would mislead.",
