    "difficulty": "medium",
    "year": 0,
    "content": "試說明「會展旅遊」（MICE）與一般休閒旅遊在【出行決定者】、【季節性】及【人均消費】三方面的分別，並解釋何以許多城市積極爭取會展業務。",
    "explanation": "三方面的分別之中，「出行決定者」最能解釋其餘兩項 —— 因為付款者並非出行者，價格敏感度較低，人均消費因而較高；因為行程由機構安排，故跟隨商業日程而非假期，季節性較平緩。作答時若能點出這一層因果，比三項並列更能顯示理解。\n最後一段關於代價的補充值得寫上。近年試題日益重視「政策有其成本」這一面，而場館使用率不足正是多個城市的真實經驗；只講好處而不提風險的答案，在評鑑類問題中通常無法取得最高一檔的分數。",
    "referenceAnswer": "三方面的分別：\n出行決定者 —— 休閒旅遊由旅客本人決定行程、目的地與預算；會展旅遊的出行決定多由僱主、行業組織或主辦機構作出，出行者本人選擇有限，付款者亦往往並非出行者本人。\n季節性 —— 休閒旅遊高度集中於假期與旺季，淡旺季差距顯著；會展活動則按商業與行業日程安排，多在平日及非旅遊旺季舉行，季節波動相對平緩。\n人均消費 —— 會展旅客的人均消費一般顯著高於休閒旅客。原因包括：由機構付費，故對價格較不敏感；住宿多選市區高級酒店；停留期間另有宴會、場地租用、展覽搭建、商務餐飲等支出；部分與會者會於會期前後延長逗留作觀光。\n\n何以城市積極爭取：\n其一，填補淡季。會展活動多在旅遊淡季及平日舉行，可平衡酒店與航空的季節性空置，令設施全年使用率提高，而非在旺季擠迫、淡季閒置。\n其二，帶動範圍廣。一場大型會展的支出不只落在酒店，尚涵蓋場地、餐飲、交通、翻譯、搭建、印刷、保安等多個行業，經濟效益的擴散面較休閒旅遊為闊。\n其三，具長遠效應。與會者多為行業內的專業人士，其對該城市的印象可能轉化為日後的投資、合作或重訪；成功主辦亦提升城市在該行業的地位，有助爭取往後的活動。\n須留意，爭取會展業務亦有代價：場館建設與申辦的投入巨大，而回報須以長期使用率計算；若場館使用率不足，設施本身即成為財政負擔。",
    "marks": 1,
    "referenceAnswerEn": "Who decides: leisure travellers choose their own itinerary, destination and budget; MICE trips are largely decided by an employer, trade body or organiser, the traveller has limited choice, and the payer is often not the traveller.\nSeasonality: leisure travel concentrates in holidays and peak seasons with a wide swing between peak and trough; MICE follows business and industry calendars, largely on weekdays and outside peak tourist seasons, so its seasonal variation is flatter.\nSpending per head: MICE visitors generally spend markedly more, because an institution pays and price sensitivity is lower, accommodation tends to be upscale and central, and the stay carries banqueting, venue hire, exhibition build, and business dining, with some delegates extending the trip for sightseeing.\nWhy cities compete: first, it fills the trough, balancing seasonal vacancy in hotels and airlines and raising year-round utilisation instead of crowding in peak season and idling out of it. Second, its reach is wide: a major event spends not only on hotels but on venues, catering, transport, interpreting, construction, printing and security, so the economic spread is broader than leisure tourism. Third, it has long-run effects: delegates are professionals whose impressions may translate into later investment, partnership or return visits, and a successful event raises the city’s standing in that sector and helps win future business.\nThere are costs: venues and bids require large outlays whose return depends on long-run utilisation, and an underused venue becomes a fiscal burden in itself.",
    "contentEn": "Explain how MICE travel differs from leisure travel in who decides the trip, seasonality and spending per head, and why many cities compete for MICE business.",
    "explanationEn": "Of the three differences, who decides explains the other two: because the payer is not the traveller, price sensitivity is lower and spending higher; because an institution sets the schedule, travel follows business calendars rather than holidays and seasonality flattens. Drawing that causal link shows more understanding than three parallel statements.\nThe closing note on cost is worth including. Recent papers increasingly test the recognition that policies carry costs, and underused venues are a real experience in several cities; an answer listing only benefits rarely reaches the top band in evaluative questions."
